{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1556518005,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0MzgxNjExODU=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3359","origin":"github"},"title":"Extractor for `run` provenance","message":"In response to #3358 we should use PROV-JSON (https://www.w3.org/Submission/2013/SUBM-prov-json-20130424/) to report on information encoded in `run` records.\n\nPrimarily this will be about \"derivations\" https://www.w3.org/TR/2013/REC-prov-dm-20130430/#term-Derivation and http://www.w3.org/TR/2013/REC-prov-dm-20130430/#term-Generation (we'll have to see how reliably we can distinguish the two).\n\nTechnically, we likely want to discover all run-records first, and then look for any file in the described state that was produced by any of the discovered run-records (as the last modification, ideally while ignoring plain renaming). I think this comes down to one `git-log` call per file in question.\n\nThe new extractor implementation coming via https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/84 also needs to be adjusted to report potentially annex run-record files as \"content needed\".\n\nIt seems sensible to split the provenance information into two components\n\n1. A list of recorded \"activities\" that becomes part of the dataset metadata, where each activity is identified by the run-record's commit SHA\n2. A \"wasGeneratedBy\" metadata property that is attached to each relevant file and references the respective activity by its commit SHA identifier.","files":null}]}